          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My mother has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, is on four heart-failure medicines, and her ankles are swelling again. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about those drug classes?",
          "answer": "**How urgent:** The urgency check found no emergency pattern in the red-flag list. It rated the situation routine. That check covers only a fixed list of symptoms, so it is not a clearance. Ankle swelling that has come back in someone with heart failure is worth prompt contact with her care team, ideally within the next day or so, and sooner if it is worsening. Her clinician can judge whether her current treatment needs review. (Source: symptom risk check.)\n\n**Seek emergency care right away** if she has trouble breathing, especially when lying flat or waking at night, chest pain, fainting, new confusion, or a rapid gain in weight. (This advice comes from general safety guidance in this assistant, not from the retrieved passages.)\n\n**About the drug classes:** The guidance I could retrieve says that for reduced ejection fraction (40 percent or below), current guidance describes four foundational drug classes, usually started together or in quick succession rather than added slowly over months. The passage I received cuts off before it names the four classes, so I cannot list them from these sources. It does say that the SGLT-2 inhibitors are the class with benefit in the preserved group, and a separate diabetes guideline says that in heart failure, particularly with reduced ejection fraction, agents with proven organ benefit are preferred. (Sources: \"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: the four foundational therapy classes\"; \"Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management.\")\n\nHer clinician or pharmacist can explain which four drugs she takes, what each one is for, and whether the swelling relates to them or to something else. Please don't change or stop any medicine on your own.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
